\description{
Constrained ordination of samples from their feature abundances in the
chi-square metric: the axes of correspondence analysis that sample
metadata explain, followed by correspondence analysis axes of what they
leave unexplained (ter Braak 1986). Gives the same results as
\code{vegan::cca()}, computed straight from sparse input without forming any
\eqn{n \times n}{n x n} or dense table, so it takes far less time and
memory for large datasets. With \code{Condition()} terms, partial CCA.
}
\details{
This is canonical \strong{correspondence} analysis, a unimodal method for
species abundances. It is not canonical \emph{correlation} analysis, which
scikit-learn, MultivariateStats.jl and linfa also call "CCA".

\strong{Method}

With \eqn{P} the table divided by its grand total, and \eqn{r} and
\eqn{c} its row and column sums (the sample and feature weights), CCA
(ter Braak 1986; Legendre & Legendre 2012, §11.2) analyzes the matrix of
chi-square residuals \eqn{\bar{Q} = D_r^{-1/2} (P - rc^T) D_c^{-1/2}},
as correspondence analysis does (§9.2). The constraints are centered and
regressed with the weights \eqn{r}. With \eqn{H} the projection onto the
weighted constraints, after removing any conditions, the constrained
axes are the principal components of \eqn{H\bar{Q}}, and the
unconstrained axes those of \eqn{(I - H)\bar{Q}}. Site scores are
divided by \eqn{\sqrt{r}}, and species scores by \eqn{\sqrt{c}}.

\code{cca()} shares its solver with \code{\link[=rda]{rda()}} and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}: the constrained
axes come from a small eigenproblem with one row per constraint column,
and the unconstrained axes, when needed, from block Lanczos iteration.
Both need only products of \eqn{\bar{Q}} with a few vectors at a time.
\eqn{\bar{Q}} is a sparse matrix less a rank-one term, so these are
computed in parallel from the table's non-zero values alone, and a
sparse table is never made dense.

\strong{CCA or RDA?}

CCA and correspondence analysis assume that each species responds
unimodally to the gradients, peaking at some optimum; RDA and PCA assume
linear responses. Over long gradients, where many species are absent
from much of the range, unimodal methods suit better; over short ones,
linear methods on transformed abundances (tb-RDA:
\code{rda(counts, ..., norm = 'hellinger')}) are often preferred (Legendre &
Gallagher 2001). The chi-square metric gives rare species a large
weight, so CCA can be sensitive to them.

\strong{Adjusted R-squared}

Ezekiel's adjustment, which \code{\link[=rda]{rda()}} uses, is biased for CCA (Peres-Neto
et al. 2006). So, as \code{vegan::RsquareAdj()} does, \code{cca()} estimates it by
permutation: \eqn{R^2_{adj} = 1 - (1 - R^2) / (1 - \bar{R}^2_{perm})},
where \eqn{\bar{R}^2_{perm}} is the mean \eqn{R^2} over \code{permutations}
random permutations of the samples (their rows of the table, against the
constraints). It is random: the same \code{seed} gives the same value, and
a \code{permutations} matrix the same value as
\code{vegan::RsquareAdj(x, permutations = matrix)}.

For partial CCA, as in \code{vegan}, it is the adjusted \eqn{R^2} of the
constraints and conditions together less that of the conditions alone
(Peres-Neto et al. 2006). Each is estimated as above, but permuting the
rows of the chi-square matrix \eqn{\bar{Q}} against the fixed
(weighted) design, as \code{vegan::RsquareAdj()} does for partial models.

\strong{Matching vegan}

\code{cca()} gives the same scores, eigenvalues, inertia, \eqn{R^2}, biplot
scores, centroids and species scores as \code{vegan::cca()}, up to the signs
of the axes. Columns of the model matrix that are linear combinations of
earlier ones are dropped, with a message, as in \code{vegan}. Samples with no
observations are an error, as in \code{vegan}; features with no observations
have no weight, and get species scores of 0.

With no constraints, \code{cca(counts)} is a correspondence analysis, as
\code{vegan::cca(counts)}, and the same as \code{\link[=ca]{ca()}}.

\strong{Permutation tests}

\code{cca()} has no permutation test of its own; \code{vegan::anova.cca()} gives
one. (Unlike RDA's, CCA's test is not a PERMANOVA of any distance.)

\strong{Same names in other packages}

\code{vegan::cca()} shares its name with ecodive's \code{cca()}, and whichever
package was attached last masks the other's. Write \code{ecodive::cca()}, or
use \code{\link[=count_ord]{count_ord()}}, which always runs ecodive's, to be sure.

\strong{Axis signs and reproducibility}

An eigenvector's sign is arbitrary, so different software can return
mirror images of the same axis. \code{cca()} flips each axis so that its
largest score in absolute value is positive, and flips the biplot scores,
centroids and loadings with it. Results are identical from run to run
and for any number of \code{cpus}.
}
\section{Ordination result}{

Every ordination function (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}},
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}}, \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, \code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, and so
\code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}) returns the same kind of
object: a numeric matrix with one row per sample and one column per
axis, so that it can go straight into \code{plot()} or a data frame. Rows are
named after the samples, when the input has names. Columns are named
after the method: \code{"PCoA1"}, \code{"PC1"}, \code{"RPCA1"}, \code{"dbRDA1"} or \code{"CAP1"}
(then \code{"MDS1"} for unconstrained axes), \code{"MDS1"}, \code{"NMDS1"},
\code{"Isomap1"}, \code{"TSNE1"}, \code{"UMAP1"}, and so on.

Its attributes always begin with these three, in this order, each with
one value per column:
\itemize{
\item \code{method}: The method's ID, e.g. \code{"pcoa"} or \code{"nmds"}.
\item \code{eig}: Each axis's eigenvalue, on the method's own scale: \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}
and \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}} give the eigenvalues of Gower's centered matrix (sums
of squares); \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}} give variances, which are \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s
divided by \eqn{n - 1} for \eqn{n} samples; and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} and
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}} follow \code{vegan}, which also divides by \eqn{n - 1} when any
distance is 4 or more. \code{NA} for \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, and
\code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, which have no eigenvalues.
\item \code{prop}: The proportion of the total variance (or inertia) that each
axis explains. Unlike \code{eig}, it is comparable between methods, so use
it to label axes. \code{NA} where it isn't defined.
}

Attributes particular to the method follow them. The matrix has class
\code{"ecodive_ord"} (as well as \code{"matrix"}), only so that \code{print()} lists the
other attributes by name rather than in full. Subsetting with \code{[}, or
\code{as.data.frame()}, gives a plain matrix or data frame without them.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\examples{
    counts <- round(iris[, 1:4] * 10)
    ord    <- cca(counts, 'Species', iris)
    plot(ord, col = iris$Species)
    attr(ord, 'r_squared')
    attr(ord, 'loadings')

    # vegan's style, with a numeric constraint.
    ord <- cca(counts ~ Petal.Width, iris, n_axes = 2)
    str(ord)

    # Without constraints, a correspondence analysis.
    head(cca(counts))

}
